# ~/.claude/.governance-local.env — machine-local values for the governance framework.
#
# MACHINE-LOCAL. Never committed, never synced, never shipped in the bundle.
# sync-governance-copies.sh only copies paths containing `.claude/hooks/governance/` or
# `.claude/skills/`, so a file at the ~/.claude ROOT is out of its reach by construction —
# the same protection `.governance-mirrors` and `.pii-names` rely on.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (2026-09-01). Tracked governance files used to carry this machine's
# real values in their CONTENT: real phone numbers, a third party's number, private checkout
# paths, the owner's home directory, a business domain. The PostToolUse sync copied them into
# the installer bundle and the Stop hook pushed that bundle to a PUBLIC GitHub repo —
# automatically, with no sanitization step and no human anywhere in the loop.
#
# The fix is not to break the pipe. A pipe that carries clean data is useful and stays; the
# same sync that spread the contamination is what will spread the fix. The fix is that tracked
# files carry PLACEHOLDERS or ENV LOOKUPS, and the real values live here, where the pipe
# cannot reach them. That also makes `install.sh --force` safe to overwrite everything,
# because nothing personal is left in the files it overwrites.
#
# See GOTCHAS #346 and ~/.claude/docs/GOVERNANCE-AGENT-GUIDE.md section 3.
#
# FORMAT: KEY=value, one per line. Consumers read these as ENVIRONMENT variables, so export
# them from your shell profile — defining them here alone does not put them in a process
# environment. Keep that in mind before concluding a value "is set".

# --- Governance repo location ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Read by end-session.sh as ${GOV_REPO_PATH:-$HOME/claude-code-governance}. That hook clones,
# commits and pushes the public framework repo, so it needs a real path — and a real path must
# never be hardcoded in a file that itself ships publicly. It used to read
# "/c/tmp/claude-code-governance": a temp directory, naming this machine, inside the public
# bundle. The repo was relocated to a permanent home on 2026-09-01, which is almost certainly
# how ~/.claude/governance-installer lost its .git in the first place.
# GOV_REPO_PATH=

# --- Commit identity for the PUBLIC framework repo -------------------------------------------
# end-session.sh commits and pushes on your behalf, so whatever identity git resolves gets
# baked into a public commit log permanently. Setting it HERE rather than in the hook keeps a
# real address out of the file that ships in the bundle. Verified 2026-09-01: the existing
# public commits carry only noreply addresses, so nothing has leaked — this pins that on
# purpose instead of leaving it to whatever `git config` happens to hold on the day.
# NEVER put a real mailbox here; use the GitHub noreply form.
# GOV_GIT_AUTHOR_NAME=
# GOV_GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL=

# --- Optional: the WhatsApp/Slack bridge -----------------------------------------------------
# Fill these ONLY if you use the bridge on this machine. Every consumer fails LOUDLY rather
# than silently when one is unset — deliberately: a missing recipient must announce itself,
# never quietly send nowhere (a 2026-07-25 incident: Slack replies vanished with no error).
#
# TEAMMATE_WA_JID — recipient for skills/wa-cc-bridge/agent/remind-teammate.js.
#   RENAMED 2026-09-01 from a variable named after a real person, whose name was in the
#   filename, the variable and four log prefixes of a PUBLIC repo. If you still export the old
#   name, the script exits 2 with a migration notice. Format: <digits>@s.whatsapp.net
# TEAMMATE_WA_JID=
#
# WA_AGENT_SLACK_SEND_JS — absolute path to your slack-send.js. Unset simply means "no Slack
#   sender configured"; the WhatsApp path is unaffected. A path that is set but does not
#   resolve is reported loudly rather than dropping the reply.
# WA_AGENT_SLACK_SEND_JS=
#
# NOT HERE ON PURPOSE: per-project WhatsApp routing lives in ~/.claude/.wa-bridge.json, which
# already maps each checkout to its own group. Do NOT set CC_AGENT_GROUP — it OVERRIDES that
# per-project routing and is the easiest way to send one project's progress into another
# project's group.

# --- Optional: PII scanner -------------------------------------------------------------------
# GOV_PII_NAMES overrides the default ~/.claude/.pii-names proper-noun denylist. That list was
# ABSENT until 2026-09-01, which left the NAME_DENY rule inert while every scan still printed
# PASS — three sanitization rounds certified a tree that the one proper-noun rule had never
# examined. If a scan reports "name list: none", the scan is partial, whatever else it says.
# GOV_PII_NAMES=

# --- The project that governance-selftest.sh audits ------------------------------------------
# governance-selftest.sh part (b) recomputes every countable claim a project's documents make.
# With this UNSET it audits nothing, and selftest-advisory-stop.sh then reports GREEN-PARTIAL
# rather than GREEN — deliberately, because a green produced by verifying nothing is the exact
# defect this framework was built to end. Set it to the ABSOLUTE root of your canonical checkout.
# GOV_SELFTEST_PROJECT=

# --- Extra repositories the closing report should cover ---------------------------------------
# close-report.sh reports commit/dirty/pushed state for the project it was invoked on, plus
# GOV_REPO_PATH above, plus anything listed here (';' or newline separated, absolute paths).
# Work regularly spans two repos, and "committed here" has been true of one and false of the
# other in the same breath — which is why the report measures every one of them rather than
# asking you to remember.
# GOV_CLOSE_REPORT_REPOS=
